lessly disregardful of right or of consequences: applied both to persons and to their acts.
{{smaller block|<poem>The ''wanton'' troopers riding by
Have shot my fawn, and it will dye.</poem>
{{right|''Marvell'', Nymph Complaining for Death of her Fawn.}}}}
'''3.''' Wild; unruly; loose; unrestrained.
{{smaller block|<poem>And take good hede bi wisdom & resoun
That bi no ''wantowne'' lauȝinge thou do noon offence
To-fore thi souereyne while he is in presence.</poem>
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 27.}}
<poem>She, as a veil, down to the slender waist
Her unadorned golden tresses wore
Dishevel’d, but in ''wanton'' ringlets waved.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., iv. 304.}}
<poem>How does your tongue grow ''wanton'' in her praise!</poem>
{{right|''Addison'', Cato, i. 5.}}}}
'''4.''' Playful; sportive; frolicsome.
{{smaller block|<poem>All ''wanton'' as a child, skipping and vain.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., v. 2. 771.}}
<poem>Ye valleys low, where the mild whispers rise
Of shades, and ''wanton'' winds, and gushing brooks.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', Lycidas, l. 136.}}}}
'''5.''' Rank; luxuriant.
{{smaller block|<poem>The quaint mazes in the ''wanton'' green.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., ii. 1. 99.}}
Every ungovernable passion grows ''wanton'' and luxuriant in corrupt religions. {{right|''Bacon'', Fable of Dionysius.}}}}
'''6.''' Characterized by unrestrained indulgence of the natural impulses or appetites; dissolute; licentious.
{{smaller block|<poem>The proud day,
Attended with the pleasures of the world,
Is all too ''wanton'' and too full of gawds.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, iii. 3. 36.}}
<poem>Men, grown ''wanton'' by prosperity,
Study’d new arts of luxury and ease.</poem>
{{right|''Roscommon'', tr. of Horace’s Art of Poetry.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|''Wanton'' professor and damnable apostate.
{{right|''Bunyan'', Pilgrim’s Progress, i.}}}}

'''7.''' Particularly, unchaste; lascivious; libidinous; lustful; lewd.
{{smaller block|<poem>Thou art . . . froward by nature, enemy to peace,
Lascivious, ''wanton.'' ''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., iii. 1. 19.</poem>}}
{{smaller block|<poem>A ''wanton'' mistress is a common sewer.</poem>
{{right|''Ford'', Lady’s Trial, i. 2.}}}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' A pampered, petted creature; one spoiled by fondness or indulgence; also, a frolicsome, roving, sportive creature; a trifler: used sometimes as a term of endearment.
{{smaller block|Thy parents made thee a ''wanton'' with too much cockering.
{{right|''Lyly'', Euphues, Anat. of Wit, p. 36.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Shall a beardless boy,
A cocker’d silken ''wanton'', brave our fields?</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, v. 1. 70.}}}}

'''2.''' A lewd person; a lascivious man or woman.
{{smaller block|If ye be set on pleasure, or disposed to ''wantons'', ye shall have ministers enough to be furtherers and instruments of it.
{{right|''Latimer'', 2d Sermon bef. Edw. VI., 1550.}}}}

'''wanton''' (won′tọn), ''v.'' [< ''wanton'', ''a.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To revel; frolic unrestrainedly; sport.
{{smaller block|<poem>When, like some childish wench, she loosely ''wantoning''
With tricks and giddy turns seems to inisle the shore.</poem>
{{right|''Drayton'', Polyolbion, ii. 174.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Nature here
''Wanton’d'' as in her prime. ''Milton'', P. L., v. 294.</poem>}}
{{smaller block|Her cap-strings ''wantoned'' in front of her in the rising wind.
{{right|''Mrs. Oliphant'', May, ii.}}}}

'''2.''' To sport or dally in lewdness; sport lasciviously.

readings.c2s1:
¶
'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1†.''' To make wanton.
{{smaller block|If he does win, it ''wantons'' him with over-plus, and enters him into new ways of expence. ''Feltham'', Resolves, ii. 58.}}
'''2.''' To spend or waste in wantonness.
{{smaller block|Hee ''wantons'' away his life foolishly that, when he is well, will take physick to make him sick.
{{right|''Bp. Hall'', Defeat of Cruelty.}}}}

'''wantonhead†''', '''wantonhood†''' (won′tọn-hed, -hu̇d), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wantounhede''; < ''wanton'' + ''-head'', ''-hood''.] Wantonness.

'''wantoning'''<sup>1</sup> (won′tọn-ing), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''wanton'', ''v.''] The act of playing the wanton.

'''wantoning'''<sup>2</sup> (won′tọn-ing), ''n.'' [< ''wanton'' + ''-ing''<sup>3</sup>.] A wanton; a dallier.
{{smaller block|<poem>But, since, I saw it painted on fame’s wings
The Muses to be woxen ''wantonings''.</poem>
{{right|''Bp. Hall'', Satires, I. ii. 34.}}}}

'''wantonize†''' (won′tọn-īz), ''v. i.'' [< ''wanton'' + ''-ize''.] To frolic; sport; dally; wanton.
{{smaller block|<poem>That broad and glaring way wherein
Wild sinners find full space to ''wantonize''.</poem>
{{right|''J. Beaumont'', Psyche, i. 72.}}}}

'''wantonly''' (won′tọn-li), ''adv.'' [< ''wanton'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] In a wanton manner. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') Recklessly; unadvisedly; thoughtlessly; without regard for right or consequences.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>A plague so little to be fear’d
As to be ''wantonly'' incurr’d.</poem>
{{right|''Cowper'', Mutual Forbearance.}}
No nation will ''wantonly'' go to war with another if it has nothing to gain thereby. ''Irving'', Knickerbocker, p. 289.}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Frolicsomely; sportfully; gaily; playfully; carelessly.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>How sweet these solitary places are! how ''wantonly''
The wind blows through the leaves, and courts and plays with ’em!</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Pilgrim, v. 4.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
(c) Lewdly; lasciviously.

¶
'''wantonness''' (won′ton-nes), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wantownesse''; < ''wanton'' + ''-ness''.] '''1.''' The state or character of being wanton, in any sense.

{{smaller block|Somwhat he lipsed for his ''wantownesse'',<br>To make his English swete upon his tonge.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Gen. Prol. to C. T., l. 264.}}
I rather will suspect the sun with cold<br>Than thee with ''wantonness''.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., iv. 4. 8.}}
''Wantonness'' and luxury, the wonted companions of plenty, grow up as fast. ''Milton'', Hist. Eng., iii.}}

'''2.''' A wanton or outrageous act.

{{smaller block|It were a ''wantonness'', and would demand<br>Severe reproof. ''Wordsworth'', Excursion, i.}}

'''wantrust†''', ''n.'' [< ME. ''wantrust'' (= MD. ''wantroost''); < ''wan-'' + ''trust''<sup>1</sup>, q. v.] Distrust.

{{smaller block|O ''wantrust''! ful of fals suspeccioun.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Manciple’s Tale, l. 177.}}}}

'''wantsome†''' (wônt′sum), ''a.'' [< ME. ''wantsum''; < ''want''<sup>1</sup> + ''-some''.] Poor; needy. ''Ormulum'', l. 14824.

'''wantwit†''' (wônt′wit), ''n.'' [< ''want''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'', + obj. ''wit''.] One destitute of wit or sense; a fool.

{{smaller block|Such a ''want-wit'' sadness makes of me<br>That I have much ado to know myself.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., i. 1. 6.}}}}

'''wanty<sup>1</sup>''' (won′ti), ''n.''; pl. ''wanties'' (-tiz). [Origin uncertain.] A leather tie or rope; a short wagon-rope; a rope used for binding a load upon the back of a beast. [Local, Eng.]

'''wanty<sup>2</sup>†''' (won′ti), ''n.''; pl. ''wanties'' (-tiz). [Dim. of ''want''<sup>3</sup>.] A mole; a moldwarp.

